Hi Jared / CSOC,

The source server wpa-1cpm002.prod.mtb.com is a CyberArk CPM server, so Kerberos authentication activity originating from this host can be associated with normal CyberArk password-management operations.

When the CPM performs password verification, change, or reconciliation against Windows domain accounts, it attempts to authenticate the managed credential against the target/domain. This can generate Kerberos authentication traffic from the CPM. Kerberos failures can also be generated when the credential being verified does not currently match the password in Active Directory, which can occur during verification/reconciliation activity or as accounts are being migrated and validated.

Before we classify the alert as expected CyberArk activity, I would like to correlate the CSOC events with the CPM processing records.

Can CSOC please provide the following from the alert:

Exact timestamp/time range
Affected account names
Kerberos Event ID, if available
Kerberos failure/status code
Source IP recorded for wpa-1cpm002.prod.mtb.com
Destination Domain Controller(s)

I will correlate those timestamps and accounts against the CyberArk CPM Verify/Change/Reconcile activity and any account migration activity occurring during that window.

If the activity is believed to be related to Discovery, I will validate that separately against the CPM Scanner/Discovery logs, since Discovery activity should not automatically be categorized as CPM password verification activity without that correlation.

Once we have the timestamps and affected accounts, we should be able to establish whether the events correspond directly to scheduled CPM processing or account migration activity.

Thanks,
Ben
